            "caveat": "READ THE GZIPPED NUMBER. Minified bytes are what a bundle report shows and what an argument gets made with; gzipped bytes are what a user downloads, and it is the gzipped column this row should be quoted from. NO FIGURE WAS PREDICTED FOR EITHER COLUMN [FIGURE STATED IN ADVANCE]: the grouping split measured +10.15 KB minified and +2.55 KB gzipped for the analogous question (measurements/2026-08-21-bundle/groupings, row grouping-layer-diff), and there is no arithmetic that carries that across — the grouping arm pinned eight strategy classes and their closure, this one removes guards, two maps and a short loop. Saying so is the whole of the prediction. THE ROW IS A LOWER BOUND on what a split would save, and every reason points the same way: the excision leaves the `import { recordMovedFields }` lines in place, leaves `assertColumnsAreDeclared` on the membership source the consumer imported, leaves `OIMSingletonEntitySlot` alone, and leaves every doc comment of every member it removed. There is nothing in it that could make the difference larger than a real split would. THE SECOND ARM IS A COUNTERFACTUAL: no published @oimdb/core builds it, and what was taken out is listed under the arm below and in records.json. THE PROOF THE LIST IS COMPLETE is in the module table — the imports were left in, so if any recording site had been missed the bundler would have kept core/recordMovedFields.ts in the lifted arm too. THE DENOMINATOR is every name the package exports with nothing droppable, the largest available and therefore the least flattering to the percentage."
